  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 118 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nThe pattern as we head into the weekend may start to be less \nfavorable for more precipitation and continue the very warm/hot \ntemperatures. While we may not top out in the mid 100s, we're still \nlooking at high 90s to lower 100s in some areas, and for this to \ncontinue in September where normals are in upper 80s, it's still \ngoing to lead to heat impacts.  Each day from Sunday through \nThursday is now pretty much a carbon copy of each other with very \nlittle pops (5-15 percent range) to highs in the mid 90s to low 100s \nin the hot spots.  There's a small glimmer of hope for Wednesday, as \nthat's the day the upper trough over the Pacific Northwest attempts \nto track east over the north central CONUS, leading to a weak cold \nfront across the northern Panhandle, and maybe the best chance at \nenhanced pops.  But otherwise the extended looks to be above normal \non temperatures and below normal on precipitation.  \n\nWeber",
